## Quick Reference: Lesson 002

### Screen Memory

| Property | Value | Notes |
|----------|-------|-------|
| Start address | $4000 | First screen byte |
| End address | $57FF | Last screen byte |
| Total size | 6144 bytes | Display area |
| Bytes per row | 32 | One complete row |
| Pixels per byte | 8 | Horizontal |
| Total rows | 192 | But organized in thirds |
| Screen width | 256 pixels | 32 × 8 |

### Address Calculation (Simple Formula)

```
Address = $4000 + (row × 32) + column

Where:
- row = 0-23 (character row)
- column = 0-31 (byte column, each = 8 pixels)
```

**Example calculations:**
- Top-left: $4000 + (0 × 32) + 0 = $4000
- Centre: $4000 + (12 × 32) + 16 = $4190
- Bottom-right: $4000 + (23 × 32) + 31 = $57FF

### Pixel Patterns

| Value | Hex | Binary | Pattern |
|-------|-----|--------|---------|
| 0 | $00 | %00000000 | All dark |
| 1 | $01 | %00000001 | Rightmost pixel |
| 128 | $80 | %10000000 | Leftmost pixel |
| 170 | $AA | %10101010 | Checkerboard |
| 85 | $55 | %01010101 | Inverse checker |
| 255 | $FF | %11111111 | Solid bar |

### Instructions Used

| Instruction | Purpose | T-states | Bytes |
|-------------|---------|----------|-------|
| LD HL,$4000 | Load screen address | 10 | 3 |
| LD DE,nn | Load offset value | 10 | 3 |
| ADD HL,DE | Add offset to address | 11 | 1 |
| LD A,n | Load pixel pattern | 7 | 2 |
| LD (HL),A | Write to screen | 7 | 1 |

### Performance Comparison

| Operation | Assembly | BASIC | Advantage |
|-----------|----------|-------|-----------|
| Write 8 pixels | ~24 T-states | ~16000 T-states | ~650× faster |
| Calculate address | ~31 T-states | ~3000 T-states | ~100× faster |
| Full screen fill | ~50000 T-states | ~10 million T-states | ~200× faster |

---

## What You've Learnt

After completing this lesson, you can:

- ✓ Locate screen memory at $4000
- ✓ Understand Spectrum uses **bitmap display**, not character codes
- ✓ Write pixel patterns as binary values
- ✓ Calculate screen addresses for any row/column
- ✓ Use 16-bit address arithmetic (ADD HL,DE)
- ✓ Display visible patterns with direct memory writes
- ✓ Understand why Spectrum graphics are so flexible
- ✓ Appreciate the speed of direct pixel access

**Key insight:** The Spectrum gives you the pixels. Everything else is your choice.

**Next lesson:** The First Character - We'll learn to display actual letters by copying 8-byte bitmaps from ROM. Now that you understand pixels, characters will make perfect sense!

